// PENDING: from an OPEN SigmaHQ pull request. Not reviewed or merged by SigmaHQ yet.
// Pull request: #6242 update: linux anti forensics rules
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/6242
// Pull request author: swachchhanda000
// Change: new rule
// Title: Shred with Zero-Fill Flag Used to Conceal Deletion
// Sigma ID: d2b4f6a8-3c5e-4d7b-a9c1-6e8f0b2d4a5c
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Swachchhanda Shrawan Poudel (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2026-08-21 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1070.004
// Description: Detects use of shred with the -z (--zero) flag, which adds a final zero-fill pass after random
//   overwrites to hide the fact that shred was used at all. Without this flag, the random-pattern
//   overwrite can be identified forensically as evidence of shred execution. The -z flag is a
//   deliberate attempt to obscure anti-forensic activity, making it a stronger indicator of
//   malicious intent than shred alone.
// False positives:
//   - Authorized secure data sanitization in regulated environments
// References:
//   - https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/shred.1.html
//   - https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/759cff11d5b44124c5728663cebb8ed671c1c12c1ce7204fa29d852408b147c7/content
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/d826f6d9370fc59d350588c5c605409fe3c431aa/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_shred_zero_fill.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/shred" and ProcessCommandLine matches regex "\\s(-[A-ya-y]{0,2}z|--zero)"
